{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 4, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "והתעלמת", :translit "v'hit'alamt", :gloss "and you shall hide yourself"} {:he "זקן", :translit "zaken", :gloss "an elder"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :other, :note "Derives halakhic limitation from the plain sense of the verb: the reflexive hitpa'el implies one may choose to hide."}], :frame {:text "The Sages derive from this verse that an elderly scholar of dignified stature is not required to load an animal himself, for it is beneath his honor. Similarly, if a kohen's loading would require him to enter a cemetery, he is exempt. These exemptions are articulated in tractate Bava Metzia regarding the phrase 'and you shall hide yourself.'", :sources ["Bava Metzia 33a" "Sifrei Devarim 223"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :other, :note "Derives halakhic limitation from the plain sense of the verb: the reflexive hitpa'el implies one may choose to hide.", :by :selah, :ran? false}], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 22, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:38:05.588432284Z", :model "glm-5.2"}